Phase 1: Creating the developer account
Phase 2: Creating the app
Create App
You give an app name, contact email, and choose a use case or app type (for WhatsApp, "Business"/"Connect with customers through WhatsApp"). You may be asked to attach a Business Portfolio, or the flow creates one.
Backend, what is created:

Created object	Purpose
App ID (public, numeric)	Identifies your app in every OAuth/Graph call
App Secret (private)	Proves server-side that requests really come from your app; used for signing webhooks and token exchange
App record in Development mode	Only you and assigned roles (admins, developers, testers) can use it
Role assignments	You become the app Admin

Phase 3: Adding the WhatsApp product
Step 4. "Add product: WhatsApp" then "API Setup"

When you enable WhatsApp on the app, Meta provisions WhatsApp-related resources.

Backend, what happens (this is the key part):

A Meta Business Portfolio is linked or created. WhatsApp assets must belong to a business, not an individual.
A WhatsApp Business Account (WABA) is created and linked to your business and your app. The WABA is the container holding phone numbers, templates, and billing info. It gets a WABA ID.
A test phone number is provisioned and attached to that WABA. Meta owns and operates this number; you can't call it or use it in the WhatsApp app. It is a real number in WhatsApp's system, registered on the Cloud API.
A Phone Number ID is generated for that number. This is a Graph node ID, a globally unique numeric ID that identifies the registered sender inside Meta's infrastructure.
Default template hello_world is pre-approved on the test WABA so you can send immediately.
A temporary access token is generated (see Phase 4).
What the Phone Number ID really is

It is not the phone number. It is an internal identifier that maps to:

the actual phone number (e.g. +1 555 …),
its display name and verification status,
its quality rating and messaging limit tier,
its registration state on the Cloud API (which routes it to Meta's servers rather than an on-premise client),
its parent WABA.

That is why your URL uses it: graph.facebook.com/v23.0/<PHONE_NUMBER_ID>/messages. You are saying "send from this registered sender node."

Phase 4: The access token
Step 5. Temporary token (what you see in API Setup)

The dashboard shows an access token that lasts about 24 hours.

Backend:

Meta issues an OAuth 2.0 user access token for you, scoped with WhatsApp permissions: whatsapp_business_messaging (send/receive messages) and whatsapp_business_management (manage templates, numbers, WABA).
The token is an opaque string (usually starting EAA…). It carries no readable data for you. (inferred) Meta stores a server-side record: token → user ID, app ID, scopes, issue time, expiry, and revocation state. On each request they look it up (or validate a signed structure) rather than trusting the string blindly.
You can inspect any token via the Graph debug_token endpoint, which shows app, user, scopes, and expiry. That confirms the metadata lives server-side.
Why it expires

A user token is tied to your personal login session. Short life limits damage if leaked. For production it is unsuitable, since your service would break daily.

Token types
Type	Lifetime	Tied to	Use
Temporary user token	~24 h	You (a person)	Quick testing
Long-lived user token	~60 days	A person	Rarely suitable
System User token	Up to permanent	A non-human "system user" in your Business Portfolio	Production servers
Business Integration System User token	Permanent-ish	Created via Embedded Signup for clients (BSP/tech provider)	SaaS platforms onboarding customers
Step 6. Creating a permanent token (production path)
In Business Settings, Users, System Users, create a system user (admin role).
Assign assets to it: your app and your WABA, with full control.
Click Generate token, select the app, tick the two WhatsApp permissions, and choose expiry (never or 60 days).

Backend: a system user is a machine identity owned by the business, not a person. The token maps to that identity, so it is not affected by anyone's password change or leaving the company. The "assign assets" step creates the permission edges in the graph: system user → can act on → WABA → contains → phone number. Those edges are what authorization checks later.

Phase 5: Recipients and the test-number rules
Step 7. Adding recipient numbers (test mode)

In API Setup, you add up to 5 recipient numbers under "To."

Backend:

You enter the number; Meta sends a verification code via WhatsApp to it.
You enter the code; the number is added to an allowed-recipients list attached to your test WABA.
On every send, the API checks the recipient against this list. If not present, the request fails with an error (commonly "recipient phone number not in allowed list").

This exists so the free, Meta-owned test number can't be used for spam.

Phase 6: What happens when your Java code sends a message

Your HttpClient.send(...) triggers this chain:

Your server
  │  POST https://graph.facebook.com/v23.0/<PHONE_NUMBER_ID>/messages
  │  Authorization: Bearer EAA...
  ▼
1. Edge / load balancer (TLS termination, DDoS/abuse filtering)
2. Graph API gateway
     - parses API version (v23.0)
     - validates the token (exists? not expired? not revoked? which app/user?)
     - checks token scopes include whatsapp_business_messaging
3. Authorization
     - does this identity have access to this Phone Number ID / its WABA?
     - is the app in an allowed state?
4. Request validation
     - JSON schema (messaging_product, to, type, template…)
     - template name + language exist AND are approved on this WABA
     - number of parameters matches placeholders in the template
5. Policy / eligibility checks
     - recipient in allowed list (test mode)
     - recipient phone format valid, has WhatsApp
     - messaging-limit tier and rate limits for this number
     - business-initiated conversation rules (templates only, outside 24h window)
     - payment/billing status (production)
6. Message is accepted, assigned a wamid ID, and queued
  ▼
7. Immediately returns HTTP 200 with the wamid  ← your code logs "success" here
  ▼ (asynchronous from here)
8. Cloud API service hands the message to WhatsApp's messaging servers
9. Message is end-to-end encrypted (Signal protocol) for the recipient's devices
10. Delivered to the user's phone (or held until they are online)
11. Status events: sent → delivered → read (or failed)
12. If a webhook is configured, Meta POSTs these status events to your callback URL

Steps 1 to 7 are synchronous, so failures there (bad token, wrong template, not in allowed list, over rate limit) come back as HTTP errors in your response. Steps 8 to 12 happen after your response, which is why a 200 does not equal delivery.

(inferred) Steps 3 and 5 use the graph permission edges and per-number counters (quality, limits) stored in Meta's internal databases; the queue in step 6 is what makes the API fast and retry-capable.

OBJECT_MAPPER (Jackson ObjectMapper) :

This is a JSON converter. It doesn't do any networking. It converts between Java objects and JSON text.
// Java object/Map -> JSON string (for the request body)
String jsonBody = OBJECT_MAPPER.writeValueAsString(payloadMap);

// JSON string -> Java object / tree (for the response)
JsonNode root = OBJECT_MAPPER.readTree(response.body());
String messageId = root.path("messages").get(0).path("id").asText();

How they work together in your WhatsApp code

Java Map/object --OBJECT_MAPPER--> JSON string --HTTP_CLIENT--> Meta API
Meta API --HTTP_CLIENT--> JSON string --OBJECT_MAPPER--> Java object


Why a WhatsApp Business Account (WABA) can hold multiple phone numbers
A WABA is a container, not a phone line. It holds settings, templates, billing, and business verification. Phone numbers are separate resources placed inside it. Nothing stops a business from having many, and there are real practical reasons to.

Reason 1: Each number has its own messaging limit (the biggest reason)
Every phone number gets its own tier, a cap on how many unique users it can message-initiate to in 24 hours (for example 250, 1K, 10K, 100K, unlimited). This limit belongs to the number, not the WABA.
Example: Your parking app has 50,000 users across many cities. One number capped at 10K/day can't message all of them. Adding a second and third number, each with its own 10K limit, lets you reach more users per day. This is the same idea as needing more servers when one server can't handle all traffic; here it's "more numbers to raise total sending capacity."

Reason 2: Isolating quality and risk
Meta tracks a quality rating per number, based on user blocks, reports, and negative feedback. A number with bad quality can be restricted or have its tier lowered.

Example: Suppose your app sends both:
Booking confirmations (users expect these, rarely blocked)
Promotional offers (users block these more often)
If both go from the same number and the promotional messages annoy people, the whole number's quality drops, including your booking confirmations. Splitting them onto two numbers means a quality problem in one flow doesn't damage the other.

Reason 3: Different departments or products need separate identities
A large business isn't really "one thing." Its numbers often map to real organizational lines:

Number	Used for
+91 90000 00001	Order/booking confirmations
+91 90000 00002	Customer support chat
+91 90000 00003	Marketing and promotions
+91 90000 00004	A different brand or app the same company runs
Each shows a different display name to the user, so a support conversation and a promotional blast don't look like they come from the same source, which builds trust and keeps conversations organized on the user's side.

Reason 4: Regional or per-country presence
Example: If your parking app expands to another country, businesses often register a local number for that market, since local numbers get better user trust and, in some cases, are tied to local regulatory or carrier requirements.

Reason 5: Redundancy and safety
If a number is temporarily restricted, banned for policy violations, or hits its rate limit, having a backup number already registered in the same WABA lets you keep operating instead of being fully blocked while you resolve the issue with the first number.

Reason 6: Testing without touching production
Meta gives you a free test number in the WABA separate from your real production number. You test templates, webhooks, and flows on the test number without any risk to your real number's quality rating or your real customers.
